## Short answer: Logic is correct but will TLE on large inputs.

---

## Why the logic is correct

Your cycle detection using `vis` + `onPath` is the right approach. If a node is on the current DFS path and we visit it again → cycle exists → return false.

---

## Why it will TLE

Inside `dfs`, for every node you're scanning **all prerequisites** to find neighbors:

```cpp
for(int j = 0; j < prerequisites.size(); j++){  // O(E) every time
```

So total complexity = `O(V * E)` — with `numCourses=2000` and `prerequisites=5000`, that's **10 million iterations per DFS call**, called up to 2000 times = **20 billion operations**. Will TLE.

---

## The fix — build an adjacency list first:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool dfs(int i, vector<bool>& vis, vector<bool>& onPath,
             vector<vector<int>>& adj) {
        vis[i] = true;
        onPath[i] = true;

        for(int neighbor : adj[i]){  // O(neighbors) only
            if(!vis[neighbor]){
                if(dfs(neighbor, vis, onPath, adj))
                    return true;
            }
            else if(onPath[neighbor]){
                return true;
            }
        }

        onPath[i] = false;
        return false;
    }

    bool canFinish(int numCourses, vector<vector<int>>& prerequisites) {
        // BUILD ADJACENCY LIST FIRST
        vector<vector<int>> adj(numCourses);
        for(auto& p : prerequisites){
            adj[p[0]].push_back(p[1]);
        }

        vector<bool> vis(numCourses, false);
        vector<bool> onPath(numCourses, false);

        for(int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++){
            if(!vis[i]){
                if(dfs(i, vis, onPath, adj))
                    return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

---

## Comparison

| | Your code | Fixed code |
|---|---|---|
| Logic correct | ✅ | ✅ |
| Time complexity | O(V × E) | O(V + E) |
| Will TLE | ❌ Yes on large input | ✅ No |
| `sort()` needed | unnecessary | removed |

---

