## Question 1 — fork() system call

### Explanation of fork()

`fork()` is a system call in Unix/Linux that creates a new process (child) by duplicating the calling process (parent). After `fork()`:
- Returns `0` to the **child process**
- Returns **child's PID** to the parent process
- Returns `-1` if fork failed

Both processes run **independently** from the point of fork onwards, with their own memory space.

---

### Complete Code:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main() {
    pid_t pid;

    // Create child process
    pid = fork();

    // Fork failed
    if (pid < 0) {
        printf("Fork failed!\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    // Child process — prints ODD numbers
    else if (pid == 0) {
        printf("Child Process (PID: %d) — Printing ODD numbers:\n", getpid());
        for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {
            if (i % 2 != 0) {
                printf("%d ", i);
            }
        }
        printf("\nChild process finished.\n");
        exit(0);
    }

    // Parent process — prints EVEN numbers
    else {
        printf("Parent Process (PID: %d) — Printing EVEN numbers:\n", getpid());
