## fork()

### Definition:
`fork()` creates a new process by duplicating the calling process. The new process is called the **child** and the original is the **parent**. Both run the same code from the point of fork onwards but in **separate memory spaces**.

### Syntax:
```c
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>

pid_t fork(void);
```

### Return values:
```c
pid_t pid = fork();

if (pid < 0)  // fork failed
if (pid == 0) // inside child process
if (pid > 0)  // inside parent process (pid = child's PID)
```

### Simple example:
```c
pid_t pid = fork();
if (pid == 0)
    printf("I am child, PID: %d\n", getpid());
else
    printf("I am parent, PID: %d\n", getpid());
```

---

## pipe()

### Definition:
`pipe()` creates a **communication channel** between two processes — typically parent and child. Data written to one end can be read from the other end. It's **unidirectional** — data flows one way only.

### Syntax:
```c
#include <unistd.h>

int pipe(int fd[2]);
```

### The two file descriptors:
```c
int fd[2];
pipe(fd);

fd[0]  // READ end  — used to read data
fd[1]  // WRITE end — used to write data
```

### Return values:
```
 0  → success
-1  → failure
```

### Simple example — parent sends message to child:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main() {
    int fd[2];
    pid_t pid;
    char message[] = "Hello from Parent!";
    char buffer[50];

    pipe(fd);   // create pipe BEFORE fork
    pid = fork();

    if (pid == 0) {
        // CHILD — reads from pipe
        close(fd[1]);                          // close unused write end
        read(fd[0], buffer, sizeof(buffer));   // read message
        printf("Child received: %s\n", buffer);
        close(fd[0]);
        exit(0);
    }
    else {
        // PARENT — writes to pipe
        close(fd[0]);                              // close unused read end
        write(fd[1], message, strlen(message)+1); // write message
        close(fd[1]);
        wait(NULL);
        printf("Parent sent message and finished\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

**Output:**
```
Child received: Hello from Parent!
Parent sent message and finished
```

---

## Key differences:

| | fork() | pipe() |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Create a new process | Communication between processes |
| Header | `unistd.h`, `sys/types.h` | `unistd.h` |
| Returns | PID / 0 / -1 | 0 (success) / -1 (fail) |
| Direction | — | One way only (fd[1]→fd[0]) |
| Used with | Always standalone | Always with fork() |

---

## How they work together:

```
Parent Process
     |
   fork()
     |
  ┌──┴──┐
Parent  Child
  │        │
fd[1]    fd[0]
(write)  (read)
  └──pipe──┘
```

**Rule of thumb:**
- `pipe()` must be called **before** `fork()` so both parent and child inherit the same file descriptors
- Always **close the unused end** in each process — not closing unused ends can cause the program to hang waiting for data that never comes

